WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.600
I am now ready to describe the Lloyd
Algorithm for k-means clustering,

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.810
and I will use this simple data set
to describe how the Lloyd Algorithm works.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.260
This data set is almost too simple, but

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:17.580
it will help us figure out
the steps of the Lloyd Algorithm.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:24.380
The Lloyd Algorithm starts by
selecting k data points as cluster centers,

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:25.720
randomly selecting them.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.910
Here's our selection.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:29.910
After the centers are selected,

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:35.440
what is the most reasonable
assignment of data points to centers?

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.944
Of course, the best way to
assign data points to centers

00:00:38.944 --> 00:00:42.890
is to assign each data
to the closest center,

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:48.530
and this step is called the Centers to
Clusters step of the Lloyd Algorithm.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.970
Now, after we constructed clusters,
we should question

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:58.410
our wisdom in the initial choice of centers.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:00.200
Let's take a look at the blue cluster.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:04.460
Its center is definitely in
the wrong position because,

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:10.920
as we learned from the Center of Gravity
Theorem, the optimal position of centers

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.856
for every cluster should be at
the center of gravity of the cluster.

00:01:15.856 --> 00:01:18.592
And therefore,

00:01:18.592 --> 00:01:23.920
the Clusters to Centers step
of the Lloyd Algorithm

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.360
is essentially moving all centers
to the centers of clusters.

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:32.880
And after we've done it, what should we do?

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.480
Well, we will do exactly
the same we did before:

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:39.330
we once again will assign,
DataPoints to Clusters,

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:42.550
assigning each DataPoint
to its closest cluster, and

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:46.390
in this case, that's how the new
clusters will look like.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:50.400
And this is once again the Centers to
Clusters step of the Lloyd Algorithm.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:56.410
And after this is done, we once again
move centers to the center of gravity

00:01:56.410 --> 00:02:02.690
until points stop moving, and
at this stage when

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:07.580
all points stop moving, we declare
that the Lloyd Algorithm is complete.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.050
So, to summarize:

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:16.320
The Lloyd algorithm selects k arbitrary
points as cluster centers and

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:22.777
iteratively alternates between the Centers to
Clusters and Clusters to Centers steps.

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:26.920
Of course, a few questions remain.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:29.880
Will the Lloyd Algorithm ever converge?

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:34.513
If it converges, how much time
will it take, and if it converges,

00:02:34.513 --> 00:02:39.911
will it converge to the global optimal
optimum of the k-Means Clustering Problem,

00:02:39.911 --> 00:02:43.660
or to a local optimum of the
k-Means Clustering Problem?

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:47.270
Let's first prove
that the Lloyd Algorithm will converge.

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:50.945
Indeed, if a data point is
assigned to a new center,

00:02:50.945 --> 00:02:57.013
during the Centers to Clusters step, then
the squared error distortion is reduced,

00:02:57.013 --> 00:03:02.660
because the center must be closer to
the point than the previous center was.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:08.840
On the other hand,
if a center is moved during

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:14.430
the Clusters to Centers step, then the
squared error distortion is also reduced,

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:18.630
since the center of gravity of
the cluster is the only point

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:23.410
that minimizes the distortion,
by the Center of Gravity Theorem.

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:28.450
Therefore, the Lloyd Algorithm converges,
but it unfortunately

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:35.020
can converge to local minima, rather than
the global minimum, and that is why it is

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:39.470
customary to run the Lloyd Algorithm many
times, starting from different centers.

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:42.320
It also might take quite
a long time to converge.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.435
So, now equipped with the Lloyd Algorithm,
we can actually select

00:03:48.435 --> 00:03:53.850
the yeast genes that show some
variation in behavior during seven

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:59.176
points when we studied the diauxic shift.
There will be roughly 200-300

00:03:59.176 --> 00:04:04.702
genes in the set of genes that change
expression during the diauxic shift

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:11.730
because, indeed, a large share of these
genes are not implicated in the diauxic shift,

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:15.150
but 200 is already a large number, and
we want to cluster these genes.

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:20.791
These are the results of the clustering
of this set into six clusters.

00:04:20.791 --> 00:04:25.786
We can average each of the set of genes,
resulting in this plot, and

00:04:25.786 --> 00:04:29.963
you can clearly see different clustering
behavior:

00:04:29.963 --> 00:04:35.569
genes with increasing gene expression,
sometimes also after an increase

00:04:35.569 --> 00:04:40.360
there is a small decrease in gene
expression, on the top of the slide, and

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:45.670
genes with decreasing gene expression
at the bottom of this slide.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:52.520
We will learn later how these different
behaviors translate into different biology,

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.961
but now, our next step
is to introduce a new idea for

00:04:56.961 --> 00:05:01.420
clustering, which is called
soft clustering.

